package extraction

import (
	"context"
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"strings"
	"sync"

	infrallm "github.com/dnd-llm/dnd-llm/internal/infrastructure/llm"
	"github.com/dnd-llm/dnd-llm/internal/rutext"
)

// Stage 0 of the player-turn pipeline: free-form Russian input → atomic
// actions. The world's entities appear neither in the prompt nor in the
// grammar — mentions stay the player's own words — so parsing scales to any
// world size. Resolving mentions to real entities is the next stage's job.

// maxActionsPerTurn bounds how many atomic actions one message yields. The
// grammar allows any array length (bounded repetition over composite rules
// hangs the grammar compiler), so the bound is enforced here.
const maxActionsPerTurn = 3

// maxPlayerInputRunes bounds the player input quoted into the prompt.
const maxPlayerInputRunes = 500

// ParsedPlayerAction is one atomic action extracted from the player's input.
// Free-text fields carry the player's wording verbatim; empty means "not
// mentioned".
//
// Ownership transfers are structure, not surface verbs: «купить» is take with
// payment, «украсть» is take with covert, «продать» is give with payment. The
// parser distinguishes the DIRECTION (trivially lexical) and transcribes the
// modifiers; a 4B model never has to tell six synonymous verbs apart.
type ParsedPlayerAction struct {
	Action    string `json:"action" enum:"move,talk,say,observe,wait,take,give,attack,browse,other"`
	Who       string `json:"who" maxlen:"60"`
	Where     string `json:"where" maxlen:"60"`
	Topic     string `json:"topic" maxlen:"100"`
	Utterance string `json:"utterance" maxlen:"200"`
	// Payment is the price the player themselves named («за пять монет»),
	// verbatim; empty when no payment was mentioned.
	Payment string `json:"payment" maxlen:"40"`
	// Covert marks stealth wording (украсть, стянуть, незаметно) on take —
	// a semantic classification the LLM makes; the intent probe guards it
	// (including the negative cases: an open «беру» must stay covert=false).
	Covert bool `json:"covert"`
	// Target names what a free-form deed (other) is aimed at — a person OR a
	// thing/structure («стол», «таверна») — in the player's own words. The
	// parser-as-sensor: the verdict engine grounds it and computes the
	// consequences; the narrator only renders them.
	Target string `json:"target" maxlen:"60"`
	// Instrument is what the deed is done WITH («факелом», «кулаком»), the
	// player's words verbatim. The verdict engine checks it is actually at
	// hand and maps its category to an effect (fire ignites).
	Instrument string `json:"instrument" maxlen:"60"`
	// Charge is the social tone of the deed — a semantic classification of
	// the player's OWN text (needs no world knowledge, hence parser-side):
	// враждебно (удар, поджог, угроза), дружелюбно (объятие, помощь),
	// нейтрально (всё остальное).
	Charge string `json:"charge" enum:"враждебно,дружелюбно,нейтрально"`
	// Duration is how long the deed plausibly takes, classified into the closed
	// catalogue (see duration.go): миг / секунды / минута / долго. A perception
	// estimate, not mechanics — the round packer maps the bucket to game-seconds
	// and paces the world's ticks by it, so quick deeds share one round while a
	// slow one lets more world time (and reaction) pass. Empty defaults to a
	// short beat downstream.
	Duration string `json:"duration" enum:"миг,секунды,минута,долго"`
	// Modality + Trigger are L3's output (perception.go), attached AFTER extraction
	// — NOT part of the extract grammar (no json tag → grammar builder skips them;
	// they still serialize for the event log). The deed's envelope: actual (default)
	// / refrain (не…) / conditional (the deed fires only when Trigger holds) /
	// attempt (пытаюсь…) / feigned (притворяюсь…). Consumers land in С4.
	Modality string
	Trigger  string
	// InfluenceMode + InfluenceStake are the social-influence layer's output (influence.go),
	// attached AFTER extraction over the WHOLE utterance of a SPEECH act — like Modality, not
	// part of the extract grammar. Whether the player leans on the addressee to bend its will
	// (persuade/intimidate/deceive/plead; "" / none = not an attempt) and the concession
	// sought. Carried as a classified KEY so handlers consume it without re-reading raw words
	// — and so a threat phrased as a deed, whose verbatim utterance the segmenter dropped,
	// still resolves (the probe saw the whole reply).
	InfluenceMode  string
	InfluenceStake string
}

// ParsedTurn is the full parse of one player message.
type ParsedTurn struct {
	Actions []ParsedPlayerAction `json:"actions"`
	// Emotion is the player's EXPLICIT emotional state for this turn (L5), peeled off the
	// utterance before segmentation. It feeds the actor model (Stage 4), NOT the action
	// path — the deeds in Actions are already state-free. Defaults to StateCalm.
	Emotion string `json:"emotion"`
}

// PerceptionTrace records the layered parser's per-layer decisions for ONE message —
// which layers fired, which were bypassed, and what each decided. It is pure
// observability: Parse computes it for free (a handful of assignments, no extra LLM
// call) and ParseTraced surfaces it. The e2e pipeline bench renders it to verify the
// SEAMS between layers (a deed dropped by L2, a marker missed by an L3 gate); the
// /sim debug view can show why a turn parsed as it did. Routed != "" means an early
// lane (query or speech) answered the turn and the physical-deed layers were skipped.
type PerceptionTrace struct {
	Discourse    Discourse  // L1 verdict: action|query + the normalized utterance
	Emotion      string     // L5 verdict: the explicit emotion peeled off before L2 (StateCalm = none)
	StateStrip   string     // the deed remainder L5 handed L2 (state expression removed)
	Routed       string     // "" (deeds) | "query" (GM-answered) | "speech" (Ls lane)
	SpeechGate   bool       // the cheap gate decided to consult Ls
	SpeechAct    *SpeechAct // Ls verdict when it ran (nil = gate bypassed or it fell through)
	Spans        []string   // L2 deeds the utterance cut into
	CovertCue    bool       // mayBeCovert(input): the lexical half of the manner gate
}

// segmentedTurn is stage one's output: the player's message cut into verbatim
// action fragments, one per atomic action, in order. The list is grammar-free in
// length — a plain stringarray, which the grammar handles natively (only bounded
// repetition over COMPOSITE rules hangs the compiler, not a bare stringval*). The
// turn's hard cap is not baked into the type: it lives where it already did,
// maxActionsPerTurn, applied in Go by spans().
type segmentedTurn struct {
	Fragments []string `json:"fragments"`
}

// spans returns the non-empty fragments in order, capped at maxActionsPerTurn.
func (s segmentedTurn) spans() []string {
	out := make([]string, 0, len(s.Fragments))
	for _, f := range s.Fragments {
		if f = strings.TrimSpace(f); f != "" {
			out = append(out, f)
		}
	}
	if len(out) > maxActionsPerTurn {
		out = out[:maxActionsPerTurn]
	}
	return out
}

// ParseContext grounds the parser in the player's situation.
//
// Deliberately minimal: the parser TRANSCRIBES, it does not ground. Feeding it
// entity rosters invites a small model to "helpfully" replace the player's
// «трактирщица» with whatever name looks closest — stealing the resolver's job
// and getting it wrong. The single exception is the active dialogue partner,
// needed to tell "say" from "talk" and to anchor pronouns («у него»).
type ParseContext struct {
	// InConversation tells the parser a dialogue is ongoing: a bare line is a
	// "say", and pronouns about the partner parse as who=PartnerMention. The
	// parser never sees the partner's NAME or the location's name — a 4B model
	// happily substitutes any name it is shown for the words the player wrote.
	InConversation bool

	// Affordances are the role-specific speech-act kinds the addressable NPC(s)
	// support this turn — the interaction CATALOGUE the world makes available, on
	// top of the universal speak/deed. Today the only entry is "trade" (a merchant
	// is addressable). They gate the Ls grammar via a dynamic enum: the model can
	// PHYSICALLY pick trade only when a merchant is present, so a wares-question to
	// a guard cannot become trade. This is a world-PRECONDITION fact (which
	// perceptions are possible), not an entity roster — the same narrow exception
	// as InConversation, never a name the model could substitute.
	Affordances []string
}

// PartnerMention is the closed-vocabulary marker the parser uses for "the
// current dialogue partner" instead of a name. Resolution to the actual actor
// is deterministic code downstream.
const PartnerMention = "собеседник"

// EveryoneMention is the closed-vocabulary marker for "everyone present" —
// the player addressing the room («спрашиваю у всех», «эй, народ»).
// Recognizing the address is the parser-LLM's semantic job (no hand-written
// word-form list survives morphology or another language); resolution to the
// co-located actors is deterministic code downstream, same as PartnerMention.
const EveryoneMention = "все"

// ErrNoActions means the message yielded no parseable actions — gibberish or
// pure meta-text. A sentinel, not a failure: the caller turns it into a
// clarifying question instead of a raw server error.
var ErrNoActions = errors.New("no actions parsed from input")

// IntentParser turns one player message into atomic actions via two narrow
// grammar-framed LLM stages — segment (cut into fragments) then extract (parse
// one fragment at a time). It works over any SchemaCompleter — the routed
// gateway in production, a single provider in benches — so llmbench can probe
// exactly this code path.
type IntentParser struct {
	llm infrallm.SchemaCompleter
}

// NewIntentParser builds an IntentParser over an LLM completer.
func NewIntentParser(completer infrallm.SchemaCompleter) *IntentParser {
	return &IntentParser{llm: completer}
}

// Parse extracts the player's atomic actions in two narrow stages, each a
// grammar-framed LLM call doing ONE job — the project's "constrain the small
// model" rule applied to perception itself. A single all-in-one call asked the
// 4B to BOTH split a message into actions AND fill every field of every action
// at once; under piled-up phrasing (intensity + repetition + a purpose clause)
// it overloaded and spilled the whole sentence into one field. Splitting the
// job fixes that at the right level:
//
//   - segment cuts the message into verbatim action fragments and nothing more —
//     it never classifies or extracts.
//   - extractAction parses ONE clean fragment into fields, the sibling fragments
//     handed in only as CONTEXT, so a pronoun («трясу ЕЁ») resolves against a
//     name a neighbour introduced («хватаю Мирру») and a referent field receives
//     just the name, never the action text.
//
// The actions list is never empty on success: a message that segments to nothing
// still yields one fragment (the whole input), and the grammar's enum lands an
// unplaceable fragment in "other". The raw response of the last call rides along
// for latency accounting.
func (p *IntentParser) Parse(ctx context.Context, input string, pc ParseContext) (*ParsedTurn, *infrallm.Response, error) {
	pt, _, resp, err := p.parse(ctx, input, pc)
	return pt, resp, err
}

// ParseTraced is Parse plus the PerceptionTrace — the per-layer flow (which layers
// fired vs were bypassed, and each layer's verdict). Used by the e2e pipeline bench
// and the debug view; the production turn path uses Parse and discards the trace.
func (p *IntentParser) ParseTraced(ctx context.Context, input string, pc ParseContext) (*ParsedTurn, *PerceptionTrace, error) {
	pt, tr, _, err := p.parse(ctx, input, pc)
	return pt, tr, err
}

func (p *IntentParser) parse(ctx context.Context, input string, pc ParseContext) (*ParsedTurn, *PerceptionTrace, *infrallm.Response, error) {
	tr := &PerceptionTrace{}
	input = strings.TrimSpace(input)
	if input == "" {
		return nil, tr, nil, fmt.Errorf("player input is empty")
	}
	if r := []rune(input); len(r) > maxPlayerInputRunes {
		input = string(r[:maxPlayerInputRunes])
	}

	// L1 discourse (perception.go): resolve the player's own revisions («нет, сначала…»)
	// and tell an in-world action from a question. Resilient — an L1 failure must never
	// drop the turn: fall back to the raw input as a plain action.
	utterance := input
	if disc, dErr := NormalizeDiscourse(ctx, p.llm, input); dErr == nil {
		tr.Discourse = disc
		if n := strings.TrimSpace(disc.Normalized); n != "" {
			utterance = n
		}
		// A question is answered, not executed: route it as an observe deed — doObserve
		// renders the look. But ONLY outside a conversation: in dialogue a question is
		// addressed to the PARTNER («чем торгуешь?», «откуда шрам?»), a speech act the Ls
		// layer handles below — not a GM-query about the world.
		if disc.Type == "query" && !pc.InConversation {
			tr.Routed = "query"
			return &ParsedTurn{Actions: []ParsedPlayerAction{queryAction(utterance)}}, tr, nil, nil
		}
	}

	// L5 state (perception.go): peel the player's emotional state OFF the utterance BEFORE
	// segmentation. State is L5's ontological type, and L2 must never see it — else an
	// involuntary reaction (a finite feeling-verb like «холодею от страха») is cut as a
	// phantom deed. L5 names the emotion (→ actor model, Stage 4) AND returns the state-free
	// deed remainder (→ L2). Runs in the normalization stack right after L1, so the segmenter
	// receives only deeds. Resilient — on failure keep the utterance and default to calm.
	// L5 state (perception.go): TWO focused passes, run CONCURRENTLY (~one call's latency).
	// DetectState names the emotion (→ actor model); StripState peels the state expression
	// off, leaving the deed remainder (→ L2), so the segmenter never sees state — else an
	// involuntary reaction («холодею от страха») is cut as a phantom deed. Combining the two
	// into one call overloaded the 4B (benched), hence the split. Resilient: a failed pass
	// keeps its safe default (calm / the utterance unchanged).
	emotion, stripped := StateCalm, utterance
	var l5 sync.WaitGroup
	l5.Add(2)
	go func() {
		defer l5.Done()
		if e, sErr := DetectState(ctx, p.llm, utterance); sErr == nil {
			emotion = e
		}
	}()
	go func() {
		defer l5.Done()
		if d, sErr := StripState(ctx, p.llm, utterance); sErr == nil {
			stripped = d
		}
	}()
	l5.Wait()
	if s := strings.TrimSpace(stripped); s != "" {
		utterance = s
	} else {
		// State ONLY, no deed: StripState peeled the utterance to nothing, so the feeling IS the
		// turn. «прихожу в ярость» — and equally EXPRESSED CALM «пытаюсь взять себя в руки»
		// (bench: self-calming strips to empty too). Like the query (L1) and speech (Ls) lanes,
		// it routes OUT of the deed pipeline; the emotion drives the actor (self-regulation
		// contest). The EMPTY remainder is the signal there is no deed; tr.Emotion carries the
		// label, calm included, for the state lane to contest.
		//
		// KNOWN SEAM (M, NOT fixed): a DIRECTED social act «благодарю Анну» ALSO strips to empty
		// (the 4B reads warmth as a feeling), mis-routing here to a self-calming felt-bit. TWO
		// fixes failed: a StripState prompt rule (removal-test — didn't move the metric) and a
		// post-strip speech classifier (regressed «прихожу в ярость»/«беру себя в руки» → the
		// classifier HALLUCINATES an addressee for pure states, so an addressee-gate can't tell
		// them apart). Real root: StripState over-peels a directed act; the fix belongs in
		// StripState (keep an ADDRESSEED social act intact), proven via the `state` bench — a
		// dedicated bench-first L5 cycle, not a routing patch. Left unrouted rather than regress.
		tr.Routed, tr.Emotion, tr.StateStrip = "state", emotion, ""
		return &ParsedTurn{Emotion: emotion}, tr, nil, nil
	}
	tr.Emotion, tr.StateStrip = emotion, utterance

	// Ls speech act (perception.go): in dialogue — or on a speech verb / quoted line / a
	// question — the player is usually SPEAKING, a distinct ontological lane the
	// physical-deed pipeline (L1 action/query + L2 deed segment) has no slot for, which
	// is why the monolithic extract mislabelled say/browse as observe. Classify it
	// narrowly (benched: parserbench speech) over the ORIGINAL input, NOT the L1-normalized
	// text — like L3 modality, the speech act is a property of what the player ACTUALLY
	// said, and L1's normalization-for-segmentation can strip a cue the classifier needs
	// (a respectful vocative that marks a wares-inquiry to a merchant). A non-deed verdict
	// yields the speech action directly (say/talk/browse) with its addressee deixis.
	// "deed" — or any failure — falls through to the physical pipeline below.
	if tr.SpeechGate = mayBeSpeech(input, pc.InConversation); tr.SpeechGate {
		if sa, sErr := ClassifySpeechAct(ctx, p.llm, input, pc.InConversation, pc.Affordances); sErr == nil {
			tr.SpeechAct = &sa
			if sa.Kind != SpeechDeed {
				tr.Routed = "speech"
				act := speechAction(sa, pc.InConversation)
				p.attachInfluence(ctx, &act, input) // a directed speech act may carry will-bending pressure
				return &ParsedTurn{Actions: []ParsedPlayerAction{act}, Emotion: emotion}, tr, nil, nil
			}
		}
	}

	spans, resp, err := p.segment(ctx, utterance, pc)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, tr, resp, fmt.Errorf("segment player input: %w", err)
	}
	// A message that segments to nothing is still one action — the whole input;
	// a turn is never silently dropped for want of a clean cut. (spans() already
	// capped the list at maxActionsPerTurn.)
	if len(spans) == 0 {
		spans = []string{utterance}
	}
	tr.Spans = spans

	// Each fragment extracts independently (siblings are context, not parsed), so
	// the calls run concurrently: latency stays ~one extraction regardless of the
	// action count. Parsing is input perception — run once, never replayed — so
	// concurrent ordering is safe.
	actions := make([]ParsedPlayerAction, len(spans))
	resps := make([]*infrallm.Response, len(spans))
	errs := make([]error, len(spans))
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := range spans {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			a, r, e := p.extractAction(ctx, spans, i, pc)
			actions[i], resps[i], errs[i] = a, r, e
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
	for i, e := range errs {
		if e != nil {
			return nil, tr, resps[i], fmt.Errorf("extract action %d: %w", i, e)
		}
		// Anchor the model's free-text fields against THIS fragment (not the whole
		// message): a hallucinated name shares no word with the fragment it came
		// from and dissolves, so the resolver asks rather than chasing a ghost.
		sanitizeGrounding(spans[i], &actions[i])
	}

	// Per-deed envelopes (perception.go): focused probes over the ORIGINAL input (a marker
	// often sits outside the deed's own words, and L1 normalization for segmentation can
	// strip it). Each is its own ontological type / focused pass — the «один тип — один
	// слой» win that made each reliable on 4B where the monolithic extract under-read them
	// (benched: parserbench modality|covert|payment|duration):
	//   · modality (L3) — the deed's mood; runs ALWAYS. The closed modality enum it emits
	//     IS the deterministic feature consumers branch on; «есть ли модальность» is language
	//     PERCEPTION, the LLM's job, never a Go word-list (a monolingual, drifting pre-filter
	//     silently missed «едва» → conditional lost). See CLAUDE.md «Никакой привязки к словам
	//     естественного языка в ядре». The probe is concurrent with the always-on duration
	//     probe, so running it unconditionally costs ~no wall-clock — only the gate's lost
	//     throughput micro-saving, which never justified a monolingual liability.
	//   · covert (manner) — stealth; GATED on an acquisitive deed (structural) OR a stealth
	//     cue (lexical — the same word-list smell; a follow-up sweep under the rule above).
	//   · payment (terms) — a named price; GATED on a transfer deed (take/give) — a STRUCTURAL
	//     gate on the classified action enum, not on words: clean.
	//   · duration (Ld tempo) — how long the deed takes; runs ALWAYS (every deed has one).
	// Concurrent across deeds AND envelopes (each writes its OWN field — no shared field,
	// no race). Every probe defaults safely on failure, so the turn is never dropped.
	covertCue := mayBeCovert(input)
	tr.CovertCue = covertCue
	var ewg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := range actions {
		actions[i].Modality = ModalityActual // default if the probe fails
		ewg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer ewg.Done()
			if m, e := DetectModality(ctx, p.llm, input, spans[i]); e == nil {
				actions[i].Modality, actions[i].Trigger = m.Modality, m.Trigger
			}
		}(i)
		if isAcquisitive(actions[i].Action) || covertCue {
			ewg.Add(1)
			go func(i int) {
				defer ewg.Done()
				if cov, e := DetectCovert(ctx, p.llm, input, spans[i]); e == nil {
					actions[i].Covert = cov
				}
			}(i)
		}
		if actions[i].Action == "take" || actions[i].Action == "give" {
			ewg.Add(1)
			go func(i int) {
				defer ewg.Done()
				if pay, e := DetectPayment(ctx, p.llm, input, spans[i]); e == nil {
					actions[i].Payment = pay
				}
			}(i)
		}
		//   · influence (social pressure) — attachInfluence is GATED on a SPEECH act (talk/say),
		//     a STRUCTURAL gate on the classified action enum. It reads the WHOLE reply (the
		//     lever — a threat, a bargain, a lie, a plea — lives in framing the segment's
		//     verbatim utterance drops), so a will-bending demand resolves whether voiced
		//     plainly or wrapped in a deed-verb. (The speech lane attaches it on its own path.)
		ewg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer ewg.Done()
			p.attachInfluence(ctx, &actions[i], input)
		}(i)
		ewg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer ewg.Done()
			if d, e := DetectDuration(ctx, p.llm, input, spans[i]); e == nil {
				actions[i].Duration = d
			}
		}(i)
	}
	ewg.Wait()

	last := resp
	if n := len(resps); n > 0 && resps[n-1] != nil {
		last = resps[n-1]
	}
	return &ParsedTurn{Actions: actions, Emotion: emotion}, tr, last, nil
}

// attachInfluence is the social-influence envelope: for a SPEECH act (talk/say — a structural
// gate on the classified action enum) it reads the WHOLE reply and records whether the player
// leans on the addressee to bend its will (the lever often lives in framing the segment drops).
// Used by BOTH lanes that mint a speech action — the speech lane's early return and the
// physical-deed pipeline's envelope pass — so influence is perceived once, wherever speech is born.
func (p *IntentParser) attachInfluence(ctx context.Context, a *ParsedPlayerAction, input string) {
	if a.Action != "talk" && a.Action != "say" {
		return
	}
	if in, e := ClassifyInfluence(ctx, p.llm, input); e == nil {
		a.InfluenceMode, a.InfluenceStake = in.Mode, in.Stake
	}
}

// queryAction wraps a player's question as an observe deed: a query is answered, not
// executed, and doObserve already renders a targeted look (topic = the question).
func queryAction(question string) ParsedPlayerAction {
	return ParsedPlayerAction{
		Action:   "observe",
		Topic:    question,
		Charge:   "нейтрально",
		Duration: "секунды",
		Modality: ModalityActual,
	}
}

// isAcquisitive reports whether an action could be a covert acquisition — the deed
// types on which the manner (covert) probe is worth running even with no explicit
// stealth word (a stealth circumstance has open-ended wording a lexical gate can miss,
// but it only ever matters on an acquisitive deed).
func isAcquisitive(action string) bool {
	return action == "take" || action == "give" || action == "other"
}

// mayBeCovert is the lexical half of the manner gate: a stealth cue anywhere in the
// utterance (the other half is an acquisitive action type). Over-inclusive — a false
// positive costs one DetectCovert call that returns false.
func mayBeCovert(s string) bool {
	low := " " + strings.ToLower(s) + " "
	for _, m := range []string{"тих", "незаметн", "крад", "украдк", "исподтишк", "втихар", "скрытн", "тайк", "не вид", "не смотр", "не замет", "отверн", "спящ", "со спины", "украст", "стян", "стащ", "свистну", "улучив"} {
		if strings.Contains(low, m) {
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// mayBeSpeech gates the Ls speech layer: in a conversation every turn is a speech
// candidate; otherwise a speech verb, a quoted line, a question mark, or a hail marks
// one. Over-inclusive — a false positive costs one ClassifySpeechAct that returns deed
// and falls through to the physical pipeline.
func mayBeSpeech(s string, inConv bool) bool {
	if inConv {
		return true
	}
	if strings.ContainsAny(s, "?:«\"") {
		return true
	}
	low := " " + strings.ToLower(s) + " "
	for _, m := range []string{"говор", "спраш", "крич", "зов", "шепч", "отвеч", "молв", "обраща", "вопрош", "оклик", "бормоч", " эй", "народ", " ору "} {
		if strings.Contains(low, m) {
			return true
		}
	}
	return false
}

// speechAction maps an Ls verdict to a ParsedPlayerAction. trade → browse; speak → say
// in an ongoing dialogue (a line the partner reacts to) or talk when initiating
// (addressing someone with a topic). The addressee rides through as who — a name or the
// closed-vocab deixis sentinel (собеседник / все) the resolver grounds downstream.
func speechAction(sa SpeechAct, inConv bool) ParsedPlayerAction {
	a := ParsedPlayerAction{
		Who:      sa.Addressee,
		Charge:   "нейтрально",
		Duration: "секунды",
		Modality: ModalityActual,
	}
	switch sa.Kind {
	case SpeechTrade:
		a.Action = "browse"
	default: // speak
		if inConv {
			a.Action = "say"
			a.Utterance = sa.Content
		} else {
			a.Action = "talk"
			a.Topic = sa.Content
		}
	}
	return a
}

// segment is stage one: the player's message → ordered verbatim action
// fragments. Its only job is where to cut; it neither classifies nor extracts.
func (p *IntentParser) segment(ctx context.Context, input string, pc ParseContext) ([]string, *infrallm.Response, error) {
	request := infrallm.Request{
		SystemPrompt: segmentSystemPrompt,
		UserPrompt:   buildSegmentUserPrompt(input, pc),
		Temperature:  0.1, // pure transcription: where to cut, nothing invented
		MaxTokens:    400, // fragments only echo the input back, never more than it
		Language:     "ru",
	}
	seg, resp, err := infrallm.Decode[segmentedTurn](ctx, p.llm, request)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, resp, err
	}
	return seg.spans(), resp, nil
}

// extractAction is stage two: ONE fragment → its structured fields. The sibling
// fragments and the dialogue flag are context only (pronoun resolution, say-vs-
// talk), never parsed here — so a referent field gets the name the fragment
// names, not a transcription of the whole turn.
func (p *IntentParser) extractAction(ctx context.Context, spans []string, idx int, pc ParseContext) (ParsedPlayerAction, *infrallm.Response, error) {
	request := infrallm.Request{
		SystemPrompt: extractSystemPrompt,
		UserPrompt:   buildExtractUserPrompt(spans, idx, pc),
		Temperature:  0.2, // near-deterministic: extraction is transcription
		MaxTokens:    320, // one action object, eleven short fields
		Language:     "ru",
	}
	return infrallm.Decode[ParsedPlayerAction](ctx, p.llm, request)
}

// sanitizeGrounding undoes the model's favourite cheat: putting a name the
// player never wrote into who/where (pulled from training priors or any context
// it saw). A value must be anchored in the FRAGMENT this action came from —
// share a word with it — or be the explicit PartnerMention/EveryoneMention
// marker. Unanchored who/where dissolve to EMPTY: stage one already isolated the
// fragment, so an unanchored name is a hallucination, not a referent the resolver
// could chase — losing the slot makes the turn ask rather than guess. Payment
// clears the same way when unanchored — the model must not invent a price. Covert
// is deliberately NOT anchored: it is a semantic classification («стянуть» means
// stealth), exactly the LLM's job — the probe, not a hand-written stem list,
// guards its quality.
func sanitizeGrounding(fragment string, a *ParsedPlayerAction) {
	fragmentWords := rutext.ContentWords(fragment)
	anchored := func(v string) bool {
		vw := rutext.ContentWords(v)
		return len(vw) == 0 || rutext.AnyWordIn(vw, fragmentWords)
	}
	if who := strings.TrimSpace(a.Who); who != "" &&
		!strings.EqualFold(who, PartnerMention) && !strings.EqualFold(who, EveryoneMention) &&
		!anchored(a.Who) {
		a.Who = ""
	}
	if a.Where != "" && !anchored(a.Where) {
		a.Where = ""
	}
	if a.Payment != "" && !anchored(a.Payment) {
		a.Payment = ""
	}
	// Target and Instrument dissolve to EMPTY when unanchored (not to the raw
	// message): an invented instrument would hand the verdict engine a
	// capability the player never had — losing the slot is the safe failure.
	if a.Target != "" && !anchored(a.Target) {
		a.Target = ""
	}
	if a.Instrument != "" && !anchored(a.Instrument) {
		a.Instrument = ""
	}
	// Duration is a semantic estimate (like Covert/Charge), not anchored to the
	// player's words — but it MUST be a known bucket so the packer can pace the
	// turn. Coerce blanks and any off-catalogue value to the default beat.
	a.Duration = NormalizeDuration(a.Duration)
}

// segmentSystemPrompt drives stage one: cut the message into atomic action
// fragments, each made SELF-CONTAINED (an elided verb restored, a pronoun replaced by
// the name it refers to). A deed individuates by VERB × PATIENT: one verb over several
// distinct patients joined by «и» («беру меч и щит») splits per patient — «беру меч»,
// «беру щит» — so the verdict engine grounds and resolves each separately (its
// per-patient consequence model; a 4B can't be prompted out of this split anyway). The
// one guard is intra-phrase: a single thing named by several words («игральные кости»,
// «кружка эля») is ONE patient and stays whole. Seeing the whole message, it resolves
// anaphora once and well. It is faithful: it drops nothing the player named and never
// judges what is "significant" — the verdict engine's job, not perception's.
const segmentSystemPrompt = `Ты — первый шаг разбора команд в текстовой ролевой игре. Твоя задача — разрезать сообщение игрока на отдельные действия так, чтобы КАЖДОЕ читалось само по себе. Ты НЕ определяешь тип действия и НЕ извлекаешь поля — только режешь и делаешь каждый кусок самодостаточным. Верни строго JSON: {"fragments":[...]}.

## КАК РЕЗАТЬ (деяние = ГЛАГОЛ × ПАЦИЕНС)
- Каждый личный глагол-деяние игрока («я делаю»: иду, бью, беру, хватаю, говорю) — отдельный фрагмент, по порядку. Не выбрасывай ничего: подойти, повернуться — тоже деяния.
- Делит и ПАЦИЕНС: если игрок ОДНИМ глаголом действует на НЕСКОЛЬКО РАЗНЫХ предметов или людей, перечисленных через «и» («беру меч и щит», «обнимаю Мирру и Брома»), — это РАЗНЫЕ деяния, по фрагменту на каждого, с восстановленным глаголом: «беру меч», «беру щит». Ядро резолвит каждого отдельно.
- НО предмет, названный НЕСКОЛЬКИМИ СЛОВАМИ, — это ОДИН пациенс, ОДИН фрагмент: НЕ разрывай слова одного предмета. Признак+предмет («ржавый ключ») и предмет+чей/чего («головка сыра») — ОДНА вещь, а не две.
- «и» между ИНФИНИТИВАМИ («схватить и перерыть») или признаками («громко и зло») — одно целое, НЕ делит.
- БУКВАЛЬНЫЙ повтор того же глагола и его раскрутка — ОДИН фрагмент: «бью… бью снова и снова», «пинаю, не унимаясь», «кричу громче».
- ОТРИЦАНИЕ — тоже действие: «не делаю X» («не касаюсь рычага») оставь отдельным фрагментом, не выбрасывай.
- Придаточное цели/итога/условия («…чтобы согреться», «…пока не стихнет», «если враг шагнёт ближе…») — часть того же действия, отдельным фрагментом НЕ становится; модальность разберёт другой слой.

## ДЕЛАЙ ФРАГМЕНТ САМОДОСТАТОЧНЫМ
Каждый фрагмент должен читаться сам по себе: восстанови опущенный глагол (для второго предмета — «беру щит») и замени местоимение (его, её, их, него) ИМЕНЕМ из сообщения. Слова бери у игрока, лишь дополняя опущенный глагол и раскрывая местоимения.

